+#   ★ NASM HAS THREE `mov r64, imm` FORMS, NOT TWO, and the third is chosen by
+#   SIGN — which is why it stayed invisible: every immediate the no-flag build
+#   assembles is non-negative.
+#
+#       0 <= n < 2^32       b8+r imm32          5 B   zero-extends
+#       -2^31 <= n < 0      REX.W c7 /0 imm32   7 B   SIGN-extends
+#       otherwise           REX.W b8+r imm64   10 B   movabs
+#
+#   `HIGH_BASE equ 0xFFFFFFFF80000000` is the higher-half kernel base. It exceeds
+#   2^63, so as a signed integer it IS -2147483648 — the middle case. The old
+#   dispatch asked only `n >= 2^32`, which is FALSE for a negative n, so the
+#   value fell through to the 5-byte form and `mov rax, HIGH_BASE` assembled as
+#   `mov eax, 0x80000000`: rax = 0x0000000080000000, the high half silently
+#   dropped. EVERY negative immediate to a 64-bit register was wrong the same
+#   way — `mov rax, -1` zero-extended to 0xFFFFFFFF, `mov rax, -2147483649`
+#   truncated to 0x7FFFFFFF.
+#
+#   The predicate is FITS-UNSIGNED-32, never "looks like a big symbolic
+#   constant": METAL_FLAG_ABS (a low vaddr, rt offset + 0x400078) satisfies it
+#   and is CORRECT in the 5-byte form at all four of its sites. Widening this to
+#   all symbolic immediates would regress them.
+#
+#   SIZEL cannot drift: MOVSIZE's value branch is LENOF(MOVENC(...)), i.e. it
+#   measures this same encoder rather than restating the rule.
